{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ 'login.title'|trans }}{% endblock %}

{#
	No application footer here (XIV-79).

	This page is one centred card and nothing else, and the bar the base template
	puts at the foot of every page landed in the bottom-left corner of all that
	emptiness — chrome from the signed-in application, on the one page whose reader
	is not in the application yet. The version it carried is wanted; the furniture
	around it was not, so the block is emptied and the version moves into the card
	below.

	`login.title` stays in the title block above: the browser tab is a list of
	pages and does have to say which one this is.
#}
{% block footer %}{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
	<main class="signin-shell">
		<div class="card shadow-sm" style="width: 22rem;">
			<div class="card-body p-4 text-center">
				{% if chrome.logo %}
					<img src="{{ asset(chrome.logo) }}" alt="" height="112" class="mb-3">
				{% endif %}
				{#
					The hostname is the heading (XIV-79).

					There used to be an `<h1>` above this saying *Sign in*, over a card
					holding an email field, a password field and a button labelled Sign
					in. It named the page for somebody who could already see what the
					page was, which is a caption rather than a heading.

					The hostname is the line that earns it. This is multi-tenant and
					every installation's login is otherwise identical, so *which one am
					I signing into* is the only question the top of this card can
					answer — and somebody with two tabs open, or a bookmark that has
					gone stale, is asking it. Promoted rather than deleted along with
					the heading it sat under: a page with no `<h1>` is a document with
					no title as far as a screen reader's heading list is concerned, and
					this page would then have nothing to put in it.

					Drawn quietly on purpose. It is the heading structurally; visually
					it is a subtitle under the mark, and a hostname set in display type
					would shout the least interesting fact on the page at somebody who
					just wants to type their password.
				#}
				<h1 class="h6 fw-normal text-body-secondary mb-4">{{ app.request.host }}</h1>

				{% if error %}
					{# The security domain is Symfony's own, so "Invalid credentials."
					   is translated by the framework rather than by us (XIV-8). #}
					<div class="alert alert-danger py-2 small" role="alert">
						{{ error.messageKey|trans(error.messageData, 'security') }}

						{#
							An invitation that has expired or has already been used
							lands here, and the person reading it has no account to
							sign in to yet — so the framework's sentence names the
							cause and this one says what to do next (XIV-1). Without
							it the page is a form they cannot fill in.
						#}
						{% if invitation_failed %}
							<div class="mt-1">{{ 'login.invitation_failed'|trans }}</div>
						{% endif %}
					</div>
				{% endif %}

				<form method="post" action="{{ path('login') }}">
					<div class="mb-3">
						<label for="email" class="form-label">{{ 'common.email'|trans }}</label>
						<input type="email" id="email" name="email" value="{{ last_email }}"
						       class="form-control" autocomplete="username" required autofocus>
					</div>

					<div class="mb-4">
						<label for="password" class="form-label">{{ 'common.password'|trans }}</label>
						<input type="password" id="password" name="password"
						       class="form-control" autocomplete="current-password" required>
					</div>

					<input type="hidden" name="_csrf_token" value="{{ csrf_token('authenticate') }}">

					<button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary w-100">{{ 'login.submit'|trans }}</button>
				</form>

				{#
					Which version you are looking at (XIV-79).

					**The reason the base template gave for this is unchanged and was
					right:** it is the first question about any bug report and the one
					nobody can answer from memory, so it belongs on the page somebody is
					standing on when they hit the bug — including this one, which is
					exactly where "I cannot sign in" is written from, and the one page a
					reporter may never get past. Only the placement was wrong. In a
					footer it belonged to a page frame this page does not have; here it
					sits under the card it describes, which is the whole of what is on
					screen.
				#}
				<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0 mt-4">Xivi {{ app_version }}</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</main>
{% endblock %}
